I’ll inspect the pattern, metadata, and the provided tungstate guidance first, then identify candidate phases and build/refine the final GSAS-II model. I’ll keep the submitted fractions tied to the saved project and verify every output file against that model.

The chemistry points first to cobalt tungstate plus possible residual Co₃O₄/WO₃, but I’m treating that only as a hypothesis. I’m now extracting the peak fingerprint and checking the frozen structure library and available indexing/refinement interfaces so phase count is driven by the full profile.

The peak fingerprint resolves the main phase question: the three intense lines near 23.5–24.7° and the WO₃ higher-angle multiplets match monoclinic WO₃, while the independent 31.6/37.2/45.15/59.67/65.55/77.21° sequence is spinel Co₃O₄. A coarse two-phase fit improves markedly, and cobalt tungstate’s diagnostic ~30–31° doublet is absent. I’m moving to whole-pattern GSAS-II refinement of the two precursor phases and will then inspect residuals for any defensible trace phase.

The first full-profile refinement is already strong (Rwp 3.31%, versus a statistical minimum of about 1.72%) and gives roughly 74 wt% WO₃ / 26 wt% Co₃O₄. The remaining misfit is concentrated in WO₃ intensity ratios rather than unexplained peaks, so I’m testing constrained coordinate, polarization, texture, and specimen corrections before accepting the fractions. I’m also removing a redundant scale degree of freedom that made the first screening model numerically singular.

The correction tests are narrowing the model cleanly: preferred orientation is unsupported, and unconstrained thermal/roughness refinements drift to nonphysical values. I’m retaining only physically bounded coordinate shifts and fixed positive displacement parameters; two high-Uiso trial fits are converging slowly, while the bounded-coordinate trials have completed.

